    fn ordinalize(num: i32) -> String {
        let s = num.to_string();       // == std::to_string(int); i32::MIN -> "-2147483648"
        let m100 = num % 100;          // C truncating remainder; Rust `%` on i32 is identical
        if m100 == 11 || m100 == 12 || m100 == 13 {
            return s + "th";
        }
        match num % 10 {
            1 => s + "st",
            2 => s + "nd",
            3 => s + "rd",
            _ => s + "th",             // includes 0 and ALL negative remainders -1..=-9
        }
    }

CRITICAL: the remainders are taken on the SIGNED value with no abs() anywhere. Because
C/Rust integer remainder truncates toward zero, a negative num yields a non-positive
m100 and a non-positive num%10, so neither 11/12/13 nor 1/2/3 can ever match.
=> EVERY negative number gets the "th" suffix, unconditionally.
   -1 -> "-1th", -2 -> "-2th", -3 -> "-3th", -4 -> "-4th", -11 -> "-11th",
   -21 -> "-21th", -22 -> "-22th", -23 -> "-23th", -101 -> "-101th",
   -111 -> "-111th", -2147483648 -> "-2147483648th".
Zero: 0 % 100 == 0 and 0 % 10 == 0 -> "0th".

Positives follow the normal English rule:
  m100 in {11,12,13} -> th; else last digit 1->st, 2->nd, 3->rd, else th.
